---
title: A file you do not trust
description: Budgets for size, nesting and expansion, an accepting list with no default, and what each one actually stops.
---

The defaults are generous, because most callers are decoding their own data. A public
endpoint taking files from strangers should say what it will accept.

```swift
@Schema(keys: .snakeCase, formats: .all)
struct Upload {
    var title: String
    var tags: [String] = []
}

let strict = Limits(maxIssues: 20, maxDepth: 8, maxBytes: 4_096)
```

Three numbers, three different attacks.

## The parser you did not ask for

{{example:recipe.untrusted.mediaType:text}}

That is an entity-expansion attempt in XML, offered to an endpoint accepting only JSON. It
produced one issue and **never entered an XML parser**, because `accepting:` is checked
before anything reads the body.

This is why that argument is required and has no default. The cheapest defence against a
parser's edge cases is not reaching the parser. A server opts in, at the call site, in
writing, to each format a request can reach.

Worth knowing even so: had XML been on the list, the entity would have been refused anyway.
Assay's XML parser has no code path that fetches an external entity, so
[XXE](/formats/xml/#xxe-is-refused-by-construction) is not a flag you can forget to set.

## Nesting

{{example:recipe.untrusted.depth:text}}

`maxDepth` guards the stack. A recursive-descent parser meeting twenty thousand open
brackets traps rather than returning an error, and on WebAssembly the stack is small enough
that it happens sooner than you would think.

This example goes through the value model on purpose. A schema bounds its own nesting by
being a declaration — the types stop somewhere. A document whose shape you do not know is
bounded only by whoever sent it, which is exactly when the limit earns its place.

## Size

{{example:recipe.untrusted.size:text}}

Checked before a byte is parsed, so an oversized body costs a comparison rather than a
parse.

## The attacks a depth limit does not catch

Two shapes are small on disk, shallow, and enormous once expanded. Depth cannot see either,
because the expansion is in the breadth.

**Alias expansion in YAML.** A few nested anchors, each referring to the one before. 331
bytes reached 11.4 million nodes before the budget that stops it existed. The guard charges
each alias its *expanded* size against a budget derived from the input size.

**Shared objects in a binary property list.** Ten arrays of a thousand references each,
under a kilobyte, 10³⁰ nodes. No cycle, every reference to a real distinct object, and a
depth of ten. Same answer: a node budget, because a real document cannot describe more
nodes than it has bytes to describe them with.

Both are built byte by byte in the test suite rather than described. A test that asserts a
limit exists without constructing the input it bounds is a test that keeps passing when the
limit is deleted.

## Issue budget

`maxIssues` bounds the report, not the parse. A hostile file can be wrong in a million
places, and collecting a million issues — each retaining a source span — is its own denial
of service.

```swift
let d = Upload.diagnose(json: bytes, limits: strict)
if d.truncatedIssues { /* there were more than we kept */ }
```

`truncatedIssues` distinguishes "twenty problems" from "at least twenty", which matters when
you are deciding whether to show a user a list or tell them the file is broken.

## A reasonable starting point

```swift
let publicUpload = Limits(
    maxIssues: 50,          // enough to be useful, not enough to be a weapon
    maxDepth: 16,           // deeper than any hand-written document
    maxBytes: 1 << 20       // whatever your endpoint actually needs
)
```

Then the accepting list, which is the one with no default:

```swift
try Upload.parse(body: bytes, contentType: ct, accepting: [.json], limits: publicUpload)
```

## What is not defended

Assay validates and bounds; it does not sandbox. A `@Check` you write that is slow is slow
on hostile input too, and a regex rule is your regex. The budgets above cover the decoder's
own behaviour, which is the part the library can be responsible for.

